# Interactive auth without secrets in the transcript

A secret typed, echoed, or pasted during an agent-driven auth flow lands in the transcript and
every store that mines it. The pattern that avoids this: run the interactive command inside tmux
and inject the secret from the password manager without it ever passing through the conversation.

## The pattern

```bash
tmux new-session -d -s auth '<interactive-login-command>'
# wait for the prompt, then inject without echoing:
op read 'op://<vault>/<item>/<field>' | tmux load-buffer -
tmux paste-buffer -t auth
tmux send-keys -t auth Enter
```

- `op read` goes straight into a tmux buffer; the value never appears as a command argument, in
  output, or in shell history.
- For OTPs: `op item get <item> --otp | tmux load-buffer -`.
- Kill the session (`tmux kill-session -t auth`) when the flow completes; buffers die with it.

## Adaptation rule

Any interactive flow that asks for a credential gets this treatment - not only the password manager-backed
ones. Whatever the secret store (`op read`, keychain `security find-generic-password -w`, an env
var already outside the transcript), the invariant is the same: pipe it into the input channel,
never through echo, never as an argument the transcript records, and never by asking the user to
paste it into chat.
